// EXPECT_STDOUT interp=1
// Registry 221.1 #799 (the interpolation half): a call carrying a DEFAULTED
// parameter, made from inside a `spawn` body and sitting inside a STRING
// INTERPOLATION, used to fail C-codegen with `use of undeclared identifier`
// naming the CALLEE'S parameters.
//
// Mechanism, read in the generated C rather than guessed. `callnorm` (D102 F.2)
// rewrites such a call into a Block whose locals are NAMED AFTER THE CALLEE'S
// PARAMETERS -- deliberately, so default expressions resolve without a
// substitution walk. The spawn capture decision is `refs - bound`, and the two
// walkers disagreed on where to look: `collect_idents_expr` grew an
// `InterpolatedStr` arm on 2026-07-21 (a name used ONLY inside `${...}` was
// missing from the capture list -- [M-str-interp-closure-capture-miss]), while
// `collect_bound_names_expr` never got the twin arm. So a name BOUND only
// inside `${...}` never reached `bound`, the flat CU-wide `var_types` matched
// the callee's parameter names, and the emitter wrote `ctx->p = p;` into a
// function where no `p` exists. The same asymmetry, biting from the other side.
//
// Both walker arms are needed and each alone was measured inert: the
// interpolation is an ARGUMENT of `println`, so without the Call arm the walk
// never reaches it, and with the Call arm alone it stops at the string.
//
// Nothing is written to an outer `mut` from inside the spawn: that is
// E_CONCURRENT_MUT_CAPTURE by D415 section 2, and the first version of this
// fixture earned exactly that diagnostic.
module standalone.m799_default_arg_call_in_spawn

type Pol value { a int, b int, c int }

fn Pol.new() -> Pol => { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 }

fn callee(p Pol, stop Option[int] = None) -> int {
    match stop { Some(v) => p.a + v, None => p.a }
}

fn main() Io -> () {
    ro pol = Pol.new()
    supervised {
        spawn {
            // The interpolated call stands ALONE on purpose. A sibling
            // statement-level call to the same callee (`ro r = callee(pol)`)
            // is hoisted by callnorm to statement level, which puts `p` and
            // `stop` into `bound` through the Block arm that always existed --
            // and that MASKS this defect entirely. The first version of this
            // fixture had exactly that shape and passed on a control binary
            // WITHOUT the fix: green for the wrong reason.
            println("interp=${callee(pol)}")
        }
    }
}
